%N A292992 Numbers k such that 13 applications of 'Reverse and Subtract' lead to k, whereas fewer than 13 applications do not lead to k.
%C A292992 There are 13 forty-digit terms in the sequence. Terms of derived sequences can be obtained either by inserting at the center of their digits any number of 9's or by concatenating a term any number of times with itself and inserting an equal number of 0's at all junctures.
%C A292992 The terms are not all obtained from the 40-digit terms by the two constructions above. Number of 13-cycles by number of digits: 1 with 40 to 77, 2 with 78 and 79, 3 with 80. A new cycle, and thus new terms, appears with 78 digits (smallest term 119505289963402793878287314259983595020880494710036597206121712685740016404979). - _Frédéric Fichepain_, Oct 20 2026
%H A292992 Ray Chandler, <a href="/A292992/b292992.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..13</a>
%H A292992 J. H. E. Cohn, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/28-2/cohn.pdf">Palindromic differences</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 28 (1990), no. 2, 113-120.
%F A292992 k = f^13(k), k <> f^i(k) for i < 13, where f(x) = |x - reverse(x)|.
